_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Missing error handling for URL parsing.**

`new URL(requestOrigin)` will throw a `TypeError` if `requestOrigin` is not a valid URL (e.g., `"not-a-url"` or malformed strings). This could cause unexpected behavior or crashes when processing malicious requests.


<details>
<summary>🛡️ Proposed fix: Wrap URL parsing in try-catch</summary>

```diff
 export const isValidOrigin = (
   requestOrigin: string | undefined,
   { localAddress, networkAddress }: Options
 ): boolean => {
   if (!requestOrigin || !localAddress) {
     return false;
   }

+  try {
     const localUrl = new URL(localAddress);
     const requestUrl = new URL(requestOrigin);
     if (localUrl.origin === requestUrl.origin) {
       return true;
     }

     const networkUrl = networkAddress && new URL(networkAddress);
     if (networkUrl && networkUrl.origin === requestUrl.origin) {
       return true;
     }

     return (
       requestOrigin === `${localUrl.protocol}//localhost:${localUrl.port}` ||
       requestOrigin === `${localUrl.protocol}//127.0.0.1:${localUrl.port}`
     );
+  } catch {
+    return false;
+  }
 };
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
In `@code/core/src/core-server/utils/validate-websocket.ts` around lines 13 - 14,
The URL construction for localAddress and requestOrigin can throw (TypeError) on
malformed input; wrap both new URL(localAddress) and new URL(requestOrigin) in a
try-catch inside the validate websocket function (the block containing localUrl
and requestUrl), catch parsing errors, log or trace the error, and return a safe
failure (e.g., false / reject the handshake) instead of allowing the exception
to bubble up; ensure the catch distinguishes which URL failed (localAddress vs
requestOrigin) so the log message includes the symbol name (localUrl/requestUrl)
and the original value.
```

</details>

